          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motives"} motives
            in algebraic geometry can be adapted to derived
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+Chow+motives"} category of Chow motives
            (after localizing at the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lefschetz+motive"} Lefschetz motive
          %p
            A second approach is due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+To%C3%ABn"} Bertrand Toën
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michel+Vaqui%C3%A9"} Michel Vaquié
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriele+Vezzosi"} Gabriele Vezzosi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+stable+homotopy+theory"} motivic stable homotopy theory
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative spaces
            (in the sense of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kontsevich"} Kontsevich
          %p
            There is another approach by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arne+Ostvaer"} Arne Ostvaer
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            à la
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"} Alain Connes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matilde+Marcolli"} Matilde Marcolli
            have also introduced some motivic ideas. Marcolli also has a recent collaboration with Tabuada on the algebraic side, see her
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matilde+Marcolli"} webpage
          %h2#Definition Definition
          %h3#AsUniversalAditiveInvariant As the universal additive/localizing invariant
          %p
            The definition in (
            %a{:href => "#BlumbergGepnerTabuada10"}> Blumberg-Gepner-Tabuada 10
            ) is the following.
          #Catperf.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p Write
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  (stable)
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Cat
                          %mn ∞
                          %mi stab
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_\infty^{stab}
                  for the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
                  of
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} stable (∞,1)-categories
              %li
                %p
                  (small with linear maps)
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Cat
                          %mn ∞
                          %mi ex
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_\infty^{ex}
                  for the
                  = succeed "-category" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn ∞
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} small
                  stable
                  = succeed "-categories" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                  = succeed "/colimit-preserving" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+%28infinity%2C1%29-limit"} finite (infinity,1)-limit
              %li
                %p
                  (idem-complete)
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Cat
                          %mn ∞
                          %mi perf
                        %mo ↪
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Cat
                          %mn ∞
                          %mi ex
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_\infty^{perf} \hookrightarrow Cat_\infty^{ex}
                  for the further
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+sub-%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} full sub-(infinity,1)-category
                  of the small stable
                  = succeed "-categories" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+complete+%28infinity%2C1%29-categories"} idempotent complete (infinity,1)-categories
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#BlumbergGepnerTabuada10"}> Blumberg-Gepner-Tabuada 10, def. 2.12 and above def 2.14
            )
          #IdempotentCompletion.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The inclusion from def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Catperf"}
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+sub-%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} reflective sub-(infinity,1)-category
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Cat
                      %mi perf
                    %mover
                      %mo ↪
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟵
                        %mi Idem
                    %msup
                      %mi Cat
                      %mi ex
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Cat^{perf} \stackrel{\overset{Idem}{\longleftarrow}}{\hookrightarrow} Cat^{ex}
            %p
              the reflector
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Idem
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Idem
              being
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+completion"} idempotent completion
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Passing to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ind-objects"} ind-objects
              yields an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%28infinity%2C1%29-categories"} equivalence of (infinity,1)-categories
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ind
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Cat
                      %mn ∞
                      %mi per
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mo ≃
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Pr
                      %mrow
                        %mi St
                        %mo ,
                        %mi cg
                      %mi L
                    %mo ↪
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Pr
                      %mi St
                      %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Ind \colon
                    Cat_\infty^{per}
                    \\stackrel{\simeq}{\longrightarrow}
                    Pr_{St,cg}^{L}
                    \\hookrightarrow
                    Pr_{St}^{L}
            %p
              with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} stable
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+generated+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} compactly generated (∞,1)-categories
          %p
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"}> HTT, 5.5.7
            )
          #MotitaEquivalence.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              Say that a morphism in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Cat
                      %mn ∞
                      %mi ex
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_\infty^{ex}
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalence
              if it is an
              = succeed "-equivalence," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Idem
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Idem
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} equivalence of (∞,1)-categories
              under idempotent completion, prop.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#IdempotentCompletion"}
          #ExactSequences.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              Say that a sequence in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Cat
                      %mn ∞
                      %mi ex
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_\infty^{ex}
              is (split-)exact if it is an exact sequence (…see section 5…) under idempotent completion, prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#IdempotentCompletion"}
          #additiveinvariant.num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              A functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Cat
                      %mn ∞
                      %mi ex
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_\infty^{ex} \to \mathcal{D}
              to a stable
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} presentable (∞,1)-category
              is called a
              %strong localizing invariant
              (
              %strong> additive invariant
              ) if it
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  inverts Morita equivalences, def.
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MotitaEquivalence"}
              %li
                %p
                  preserves
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-colimits"} filtered (∞,1)-colimits
              %li
                %p
                  sends (split-) exact sequences, def.
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExactSequences"}
          #BGTCharacterization.num_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              The
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mot
                      %mi add
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mot_{add}
              or
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Mot
                        %mi loc
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mot_{loc}
              %strong noncommutative motives
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+construction"} universal
              localizing/additive invariant, def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#additiveinvariant"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒰
                      %mi loc
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Cat
                      %mn ∞
                      %mi ex
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Mot
                      %mi loc
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{U}_{loc} \colon Cat_\infty^{ex} \to Mot_{loc}
                    \\,.
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒰
                      %mi add
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Cat
                      %mn ∞
                      %mi ex
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Mot
                      %mi add
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{U}_{add} \colon Cat_\infty^{ex} \to Mot_{add}
                    \\,.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#BlumbergGepnerTabuada10"}> Blumberg-Gepner-Tabuada 10, theorem 1.1, section 8
            )
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              The localization property here (be additive, invert Morita, preserve split sequences) is of the same form as that which defines the localization of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
              to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+stable+homotopy+theory"} noncommutative stable homotopy theory
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "KK-theory#UniversalCharacterization"} KK-theory – Universal characterization
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#BlumbergGepnerTabuada10"} Blumberg-Gepner-Tabuada 10, paragraph 1.5
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#RelationToAlgebraicKTheory Relation to algebraic K-theory
          .num_defn
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mo ∈
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Cat
                      %mn ∞
                      %mi stab
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{B} \in Cat_\infty^{stab}
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
              smooth and proper, hence a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+object+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} compact object
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mot
                      %mi loc
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mot_{loc}
              between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
              is given by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-connective+algebraic+K-theory"} non-connective algebraic K-theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕂
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
              of the tensor product in that there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+equivalence"} natural equivalence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Mot
                          %mi loc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒰
                      %mi loc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒰
                      %mi loc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi 𝕂
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℬ
                      %mi op
                    %mover
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mo ^
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Hom_{Mot_{loc}}(\mathcal{U}_{loc}(\mathcal{B}), \mathcal{U}_{loc}(\mathcal{A}))
                    \\simeq
                    \\mathbb{K}(\mathcal{B}^{op}\widehat \otimes \mathcal{A})
                    \\,.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#BlumbergGepnerTabuada10"}> Blumberg-Gepner-Tabuada 10
            , theorem 9.36)
          %h3#RelationToCorrespondences Relation to correspondences equipped with cocycles
          %p
            By (
            %a{:href => "#BlumbergGepnerTabuada10"}> Blumberg-Gepner-Tabuada 10, theorem 9.36
            ), the morphisms of noncommutative motives from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}
            suitably dualizable/compact are given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Maps
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒰
                    %mi loc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒰
                    %mi loc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi 𝕂
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi op
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Maps(\mathcal{U}_{loc}(\mathcal{B}), \mathcal{U}_{loc}(\mathcal{A}))
                  \\simeq
                  \\mathbb{K}(\mathcal{B}^{op}\otimes\mathcal{A})
                  \\,,
          %p
            hence by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-connective+algebraic+K-theory"} non-connective algebraic K-theory
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+tensor+product"} Deligne tensor product
            of the two categories.
          %p
            Thinking of these as categories of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+sheaves"} quasicoherent sheaves
            on some spaces (by definition in
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+algebraic+geometry"} noncommutative algebraic geometry
            = succeed "-cocycles" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕂
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence"} correspondence
            space.
          %p (…)
          %h3#RelationToChowMotives Relation to Chow motives
          %p
            The category of ordinary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chow+motives"} Chow motives
            %span.newWikiWord
              Tate motive
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Tate+motive"} ?
            essentially sits inside that of noncommutative Chow motives. This is recalled as (
            %a{:href => "#Tabuada11"}> Tabuada 11, theorem 4.6
            ). For more see (
            %a{:href => "#Tabuada11Chow"}> Tabuada 11 ChowNCG
            ).
          %p
            This relation is best understood as being exhibited by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-motives"} K-motives
          %h3#relation_to_kktheory Relation to KK-theory
          %p
            Noncommutative motives receive a universal functor from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi KK
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msub
                    %mi NCC
                    %mi dg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  KK \longrightarrow NCC_{dg}
          %p
            which is given by sending a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-category"} dg-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perfect+complexes"} perfect complexes
            over (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitalization"} unitalization
            of) its underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            (
            %a{:href => "#Mahanta13"}> Mahanta 13
            ).
          %h2#applications Applications
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tabuada"} Tabuada
                has used noncommutative motives to compute the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+homology"} cyclic homology
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    twisted projective homogeneous varieties
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/twisted+projective+homogeneous+varieties"} ?
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Brauer classes
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Brauer+class"} ?
                of the associated
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+simple+algebras"} central simple algebras
                are trivial. See (
                %a{:href => "#Tabuada13Twisted"}> Tabuada 13
                ).
            %li
              %p
                Bernardara and Tabuada have used noncommutative motives to compute the rational
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chow+groups"} Chow groups
                of certain complete
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersections"} intersections
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curves"} curves
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#BerTab13"} Bernardara-Tabuada 13
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bivariant+cohomology"}> bivariant cohomology
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bivariant+algebraic+K-theory"} bivariant algebraic K-theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+symplectic+category"} motivic symplectic category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motive"} motive
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+motive"} pure motive
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chow+motive"}> Chow motive
                      ,
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/numerical+motive"} numerical motive
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+motive"} mixed motive
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+cohomology"} motivic cohomology
          %div
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th geometric context
                  %th
                    universal additive
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bivariant+cohomology+theory"} bivariant
                    (preserves
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+exact+sequences"} split exact sequences
                  %th
                    universal localizing
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bivariant+cohomology"} bivariant
                    (preserves all
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequences"} exact sequences
                    in the middle)
                  %th universal additive invariant
                  %th universal localizing invariant
              %tbody
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+algebraic+geometry"} noncommutative algebraic geometry
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+motives"} noncommutative motives
                    %math#mathml_55f3094059c9fbdb4ade37c5d893a16a5acbd98f_1.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi Mot
                            %mi add
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mot_{add}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+motives"} noncommutative motives
                    %math#mathml_55f3094059c9fbdb4ade37c5d893a16a5acbd98f_2.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi Mot
                            %mi loc
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mot_{loc}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory"} algebraic K-theory
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-connective+algebraic+K-theory"} non-connective algebraic K-theory
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+topology"} noncommutative topology
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-theory"} KK-theory
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-theory"} E-theory
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+K-theory"} operator K-theory
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} …
          %h2#References References
          %p A survey is in
          %ul#Tabuada11
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goncalo+Tabuada"}> Goncalo Tabuada
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A guided tour through the garden of noncommutative motives
              %em Topics in Noncommutative Geometry
              Clay Mathematics Proceedings Vol 16, 2012 (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1108.3787"}> arxiv1108.3787
              );
          %p
            Discussion of
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"}> Maxim Kontsevich
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Noncommutative motives
                %a{:href => "http://www.ihes.fr/~maxim/TEXTS/ncmotives+(Skoda+notes).pdf"} pdf
                of part of the talk, notes by
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zoran+Skoda"} Zoran Skoda
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"}> Maxim Kontsevich
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Geometry and Arithmetic - Non-commutative motives
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://video.ias.edu/Geometry-and-Arithmetic-Kontsevich"} video
          %p
            The following article has the treatment of
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ludmil+Katzarkov"}> Ludmil Katzarkov
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tony+Pantev"} Tony Pantev
              = succeed "," do
                %em Hodge theoretic aspects of mirror symmetry
